INT. WITNESS PREP ROOM, SUPERIOR COURT - DAY

A windowless room. Steel table. Two plastic chairs. A water pitcher sweating on a tray.

ATTORNEY OCTAVIA BELLWEATHER-JIN (40s, severe bun) sits across from her witness, DR. SAMUEL IGWE-ROSENBERG (60s, forensic accountant, tie too tight). She slides a folder across the table. He doesn't open it.

OCTAVIA
Dr. Igwe-Rosenberg, tomorrow you take the stand.

SAMUEL
I know.

OCTAVIA
Opposing counsel will ask about the ledger.

SAMUEL
I know that too.

OCTAVIA
And you'll say what?

SAMUEL
That the ledger is incomplete.

OCTAVIA
No. You'll say it contains discrepancies that are consistent with accounting error.

SAMUEL
They are not consistent with accounting error.

OCTAVIA
You'll say they are.

SAMUEL
Ms. Bellweather-Jin, I have been a forensic accountant for thirty-one years.

OCTAVIA
Which is why they'll believe you.

SAMUEL
Because I'll lie?

OCTAVIA
Because you'll be precise.

SAMUEL
Precisely incorrect.

OCTAVIA
Precisely helpful.

He pours water into a paper cup. Holds it without drinking.

SAMUEL
What happens if I tell the truth?

OCTAVIA
Then my client loses.

SAMUEL
And if he deserves to?

OCTAVIA
It isn't my job to decide that.

SAMUEL
Then whose job is it?

OCTAVIA
A jury's.

SAMUEL
Who will rely on my testimony.

OCTAVIA
Who will rely on your credentials.

SAMUEL
They're the same thing.

OCTAVIA
Not in this room.

He sets the cup down. Pushes the folder back across the table unopened.

SAMUEL
I'll testify, Ms. Bellweather-Jin. But I'll say what the ledger says.

She looks at him a long time. Then she opens the folder, takes out a single page, and slides it to him.

OCTAVIA
Then say this.

SAMUEL
(reading)
"The ledger contains discrepancies. Their cause cannot be determined from the ledger alone."

OCTAVIA
Is that true?

SAMUEL
(a pause)
It's true.

OCTAVIA
Then it's all I'm asking.

He folds the page in half, and in half again, and puts it in his breast pocket.
